  {
    "block_id": "2016415929101062287",
    "title": "1. INTRODUCTION",
    "sub_firstblock": "High-level synthesis tools transform a behavioral description in terms of algorithm into a RT-level structural description observing several constraints. In order to produce the data path part of a digital processor the following subtasks have to be solved: scheduling, resource allocation and resource binding. This paper is concerned with the scheduling problem as a first step in the sequence of subtasks. Scheduling is the task of assigning operations to control steps optimizing a certain objective function. Several different methods for scheduling have been published. Most of them are heuristics. "
  },
  {
    "block_id": "2016415929101062292",
    "title": "2. 0/1 LP MODEL AND EXTENSIONS",
    "sub_firstblock": "We start with the notation of the 0/1 LP formulations introduced by Lee (1989). A short summary of the abbreviations should make this paper more readable. As soon as possible (ASAP) and as last as possible (ALAP) times are computed for every operation. For simplicity the 0/1 LP model in this introduction is restricted to operations with one cycle propagation delay and function units with only one operation type executable. "
  },
