  {
    "block_id": "2016411239315542054",
    "title": "1. Introduction",
    "sub_firstblock": "The rapid rise in executive pay in the United States since the 1970s, particularly relative to the wages earned by other workers, has stimulated an extensive literature on executive compensation (Hall and Liebman 1998, Murphy 1999, Hall and Murphy 2003, Bebchuk and Fried 2003). Over this period, CEOs have also become increasingly mobile across firms (Huson et al. 2001). Surprisingly, the mobility and remuneration of top managers are often studied independently and, even when they are related, the workings of the labor market for executives receive little attention.1 This paper contributes new facts on the mobility, compensation, and careers of top executives over most of the twentieth century, with the aim of integrating the labor market to our understanding of the changes in pay and turnover. This long-run perspective suggests that an evolving market for corporate executives may have played an important role in the development of the compensation and mobility of top managers. "
  },
  {
    "block_id": "2016411239315542073",
    "title": "2. Long-Run Trends in the Market for Managers",
    "sub_firstblock": "One of the main objectives of the paper is to establish the long-run trends in the market for managers, and to relate them to the changes in executive compensation over the twentieth century. This section provides a description of the data collection, briefly summarizes the long-run trends in compensation established by Frydman and Saks (2010), and presents new evidence on managerial careers. "
  },
  {
