    "sub_firstblock": "Constructing a consistent data set on the compensation and careers of executives over the twentieth century is challenging for several reasons. A first step requires identifying the names of top managers for a sizable number of firms. Commonly used data sets, such as Compustat's Executive Compensation database (ExecuComp), only provide this information for recent decades. I build on the work by Frydman and Saks (2010) who hand collect the names and remuneration of individual top executives for 101 large publicly traded U.S. corporations (corresponding to the 50 largest firms in 1940, 1960, and 1990) since the 1930s. Their sample is a good starting point for the purpose of this study because it allows me to track the compensation of individual executives over time. "
  },
  {
    "block_id": "2016411239315542083",
    "title": "2.2. New Data on Managerial Biographies",
    "sub_firstblock": "A considerable literature in sociology and economics has studied the educational background, career paths, and social status of executives at different points in time. (See Friedman and Tedlow 2003 for a comprehensive review of this literature.) Because each study uses a different design and focuses on different aspects of managerial careers, they cannot provide a systematic understanding of the long-run evolution of the market for corporate officers. To construct a consistent, longrun panel data set following the careers of top executives within the same set of firms, I collect biographical information for the three highest-paid executives of the 50 largest firms in 1960. "
