  {
    "block_id": "2016411239315542168",
    "title": "3. Theoretical Framework",
    "sub_firstblock": "The long-run trends presented in the previous section suggest that pay and managerial mobility have not changed in isolation, but rather have been accompanied by a substantial transformation in the characteristics and the careers of top managers. A plausible explanation for these patterns needs to match the following facts: the level of pay followed a J-shaped pattern, declining during the 1940s and increasing since the late 1970s; inequality among all top executives and within firms experienced a minor decline until the 1950s, and has increased rapidly since the 1980s; managerial turnover experienced a J-shaped pattern, declining until the 1950s and increasing since the 1970s, but the mobility in the earlier decades occurred when executives were young, whereas now managers are likely to switch firms fairly late in their careers; top managers used to have mostly technical or general education, but the fraction of executives with a formal education background in business has steadily increased since the 1960s; and the diversity in occupational experience has increased throughout most of the century. "
  },
  {
