  {
    "block_id": "2016411239315542251",
    "title": "4. Empirical Evidence: Skills, Pay, and Mobility",
    "sub_firstblock": "According to the model, the long-run trends in pay, inequality, and turnover may reflect the growing importance of general managerial skills over time. But has such a change in skills actually occurred? Changes in the types of formal education are suggestive of such a transformation: MBA degrees probably confer general knowledge that is useful for different industries, whereas degrees in engineering and science may be more valuable in specific industries. The increased diversity in occupational experience also seems consistent with a shift toward general managerial human capital. Individuals with general skills are arguably more likely to work successfully in different sectors. "
