  {
    "block_id": "2016411239315542295",
    "title": "5. Interpretation of the Findings",
    "sub_firstblock": "The analysis thus far takes a shift in the importance of general managerial skills as given, but investments in formal education, individuals' willingness to accumulate experience in various sectors, and firms' decisions to provide certain skills on the job are endogenous choices. Still, these choices may be an optimal response to an exogenous change in the weight of general skills in the firms' production function. A resulting question is the following: Which factors could have caused such a change in the type of skills over time? "
  },
  {
    "block_id": "2016411239315542301",
    "title": "5.1. Mechanisms Affecting the Relative Importance of General and Specific Skills",
    "sub_firstblock": "An extensive literature debates the causes for the recent rise in executive pay, but most of the proposed explanations are silent on turnover and managerial careers. A challenge for any theory is to account for all of the long-run stylized facts established in this paper. The two most popular explanations view compensation as either the result of the competitive assignment of CEOs to firms, or the outcome of rent extraction by powerful CEOs (Jenter and Frydman 2010). Interestingly, these theories have implicitly opposite views on the workings of the labor market. Next, I consider each in more detail. "
  },
  {
    "block_id": "2016411239315542312",
    "title": "5.2. Alternative Explanations",
    "sub_firstblock": "This paper establishes a series of stylized facts on managerial careers over most of the twentieth century. In combination with the long-run trends in executive pay documented by Frydman and Saks (2010), these facts provide a challenge for the most common explanations for the recent rise in compensation. I propose that the long-run trends in the level of managerial pay, inequality among managers, and the external mobility of corporate officers can be partly explained by a shift in the importance of general managerial skills. Following Murphy and Zábojnik (2004), Murphy and Zábojnik (2007), I present a simple framework to show that an increase in the importance of general skills can lead to higher levels of pay, higher levels of managerial turnover, and more inequality among top managers. "
